<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/intermediate/abnormal-psychology-therapies">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Abnormal psychology studies <strong>mental disorder: its definitions, causes and
    treatments</strong> — a field where the object of study is suffering itself. Two
    disciplines structure it. First, diagnosis: deciding what counts as a disorder, which
    requires care because the boundary is drawn by convention, culture and consequences,
    not discovered like a bone. Second, treatment evaluation: matching therapies to
    conditions with evidence rather than tradition. The modern position is diagnostic
    humility (categories are descriptions, not essences) and treatment pluralism
    (evidence-based therapy, medication, or both — matched to condition and person).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="definition, diagnosis, therapy mechanisms, and the evidence verdict" widget="equationStep" config={abnormCfg} />

  <h2>Defining "abnormal": the three Ds and their failures</h2>
  <p>
    No single criterion works alone. <strong>Statistical deviance</strong> fails because
    valuable states are rare (genius, exceptional calm) and common states can be
    disordered (mild anxiety is universal, debilitating anxiety is the problem).
    <strong>Cultural deviance</strong> fails because norms vary — hearing voices is
    pathological in London and a mark of spiritual standing in some communities — which
    is why modern diagnosis checks behaviour against the norms of the person's
    <em>own</em> culture. The working definition leans on the other two Ds:
    <strong>distress</strong> (the person suffers) and <strong>dysfunction</strong>
    (everyday functioning is impaired), with <strong>danger</strong> as a further but
    rarely sufficient sign. History shows the definition is revisable and revisited:
    homosexuality was listed as a disorder until 1973, when evidence that same-sex
    orientation causes no intrinsic dysfunction forced removal — the criterion of
    dysfunction did the correcting. The Rosenhan study (1973, "sane in insane places"),
    in which eight healthy pseudopatients were admitted after reporting one symptom, is
    the classic warning about categorical labelling — though its own reporting has since
    been contested, which is a lesson about research too.
  </p>

  <h2>The diagnostic machine: DSM and ICD</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>DSM-5-TR</strong> (American) and <strong>ICD-11</strong> (World Health
    Organization, used for billing and epidemiology worldwide) define disorders by
    <em>descriptive</em> criteria — symptom clusters, durations, impairment — deliberately
    agnostic about cause, because causes remain mostly unknown. This buys
    <strong>reliability</strong> (two clinicians applying the checklist largely agree —
    the field trials of DSM-5 showed kappa values mostly fair to good) at the cost of
    <strong>validity</strong> worries: categories may not carve nature at its joints.
    Symptoms and risk genes overlap heavily across diagnoses (<strong>comorbidity</strong>
    is the rule, not the exception — depression rides with anxiety in a large fraction of
    cases), which has pushed research towards dimensional models (HiTOP) and the NIMH's
    Research Domain Criteria, which classify by measurable processes (threat sensitivity,
    reward learning) instead of symptom menus. The clinic still needs categories to
    communicate and plan; the science increasingly thinks in dimensions. Both are true,
    and the tension is productive.
  </p>

  <h2>Schizophrenia: the most studied and least understood</h2>
  <p>
    Schizophrenia affects roughly 1 in 300 people worldwide (WHO's figure — older texts'
    "1%" counts the broader spectrum), across all cultures at similar rates, typically
    emerging in late adolescence or early adulthood. Symptoms split into
    <strong>positive</strong> (hallucinations — most often auditory; delusions; disordered
    speech) — <em>added</em> experiences; <strong>negative</strong> (avolition, flat
    affect, social withdrawal, poverty of speech) — capacities subtracted; and
    <strong>cognitive</strong> (working-memory and attention deficits, which best predict
    functional outcome). The dopamine hypothesis remains the leading pharmacological
    account — hyperactive mesolimbic dopamine drives positive symptoms, which is why
    D2-blocking antipsychotics calm hallucinations (negative symptoms respond poorly) —
    while the <strong>neurodevelopmental</strong> account gathers the risk factors:
    genetic loading, obstetric complications, urban birth, adolescent cannabis use in
    vulnerable individuals. The <strong>diathesis-stress</strong> model integrates both,
    and Tienari's Finnish adoption study is its classic evidence: adopted-away children
    of mothers with schizophrenia developed the disorder at elevated rates
    <em>mainly</em> when raised in high-conflict adoptive homes — genes set the dial;
    environment helps decide where it lands. Treatment is maintenance antipsychotics
    plus psychosocial support; CBT for psychosis helps with distressing voices and
    delusion conviction; early-intervention services measurably improve long-term
    trajectories.
  </p>

  <h2>Mood disorders: unipolar depression and bipolar disorder</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Depressive (unipolar) disorder</strong> affects around 280 million people
    (WHO). Diagnosis requires a depressive episode: two weeks or more of low mood or
    loss of pleasure (anhedonia) with changes in sleep, appetite, energy, concentration
    and self-worth. Causal theories stack in layers. The <strong>monoamine
    hypothesis</strong> came from pharmacology: reserpine (which depletes serotonin and
    noradrenaline) induced depression; the first antidepressants increased them; SSRIs
    follow — though the full story involves slower neuroplasticity changes, which
    explains why antidepressants take weeks. The <strong>cognitive account</strong>
    (Beck) identifies the cognitive triad — negative beliefs about self, world, future —
    maintained by information-processing biases, and CBT dismantles them with
    behavioural experiments; <strong>learned helplessness</strong> (Seligman) and its
    hopelessness refinement model how uncontrollable stress teaches passivity.
    Treatments: CBT and interpersonal therapy (Klerman's structured approach to role
    transitions and grief) have strong trial evidence; <strong>behavioural
    activation</strong> — re-engaging with rewarding activity first, letting mood follow
    — is the deceptively simple core that mediates much of CBT's effect; exercise has a
    real, modest effect. Roughly half of patients respond to any first treatment, and
    combining medication with therapy outperforms either alone for moderate-severe
    cases.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Bipolar disorder</strong> (bipolar I about 1% of adults) adds mania: a week
    or more of elevated, expansive or irritable mood with decreased need for sleep,
    grandiosity, pressured speech and risky behaviour — often cycling with depressive
    episodes. The clinical stakes are specific: antidepressants alone can precipitate a
    <strong>switch into mania</strong>, so treatment anchors on <strong>mood
    stabilisers</strong> — lithium remains the benchmark for relapse prevention and the
    best evidence for reducing suicide risk in the disorder. The kindling hypothesis
    (episodes beget episodes, becoming more spontaneous) captures the longitudinal
    pattern and motivates early, aggressive stabilisation.
  </p>

  <h2>Anxiety and fear-related disorders: when the alarm will not reset</h2>
  <p>
    Anxiety is adaptive threat preparation; the disorders are calibration failures,
    and they are the most prevalent psychiatric category. <strong>Generalised anxiety
    disorder</strong> is six months or more of uncontrollable worry about ordinary
    things. <strong>Panic disorder</strong> adds unexpected surges of intense fear with
    bodily symptoms — and the feared thing becomes the sensations themselves
    (interoceptive conditioning, which is why breathing retraining and exposure to
    bodily sensations work). <strong>Specific phobias</strong> show the famous
    <strong>preparedness</strong> pattern (Seligman, Öhman): snakes, spiders, heights
    and blood condition faster and resist extinction more than neutral threats —
    evolution pre-loading the fear catalogue. <strong>Social anxiety disorder</strong>
    is fear of scrutiny with safety behaviours (rehearsing, avoiding eye contact) that
    maintain it. <strong>PTSD</strong> — grouped by ICD-11 and DSM-5 with fear-related
    rather than ordinary anxiety conditions — follows trauma: intrusive re-experiencing,
    avoidance, hyperarousal, and the flashback's signature of involuntary,
    sensory-laden memory.
  </p>
  <p>
    The treatment story is the cleanest in the field: <strong>exposure therapy</strong> —
    systematic, graded confrontation with the feared stimulus (Wolpe's systematic
    desensitisation was the ancestor) — works because it enables <strong>extinction
    learning</strong>: a new inhibitory memory that the cue no longer predicts harm.
    Avoidance is the maintaining mechanism; exposure dismantles it. The modern
    inhibitory-learning refinement (Craske) reframes the goal: build multiple
    disconfirming experiences, not just "habituate". Combining exposure with
    cognitive restructuring, or with d-cycloserine as a consolidation aid, has been
    tested; the behavioural core remains the active ingredient.
  </p>

  <h2>OCD: the disorder of the escape hatch</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Obsessive-compulsive disorder</strong> (lifetime prevalence roughly 1-2%) is
    built from two linked parts: <strong>obsessions</strong> — intrusive, unwanted
    thoughts, images or urges (contamination, harm to loved ones, forbidden thoughts)
    that the person experiences as senseless and ego-dystonic — and
    <strong>compulsions</strong> — rituals (washing, checking, counting, ordering) or
    mental acts performed to neutralise the anxiety. The trap is mechanistically exact:
    the compulsion <em>works</em> — anxiety drops within seconds — and that escape
    prevents the person from ever learning the obsession is harmless, so the loop
    tightens. Imaging implicates an orbitofrontal cortex–striatum–thalamus circuit;
    the catastrophe-first framing ("my hands carry plague") is a cognitive bias, not a
    delusion, and insight is usually preserved — a distinction from the related-looking
    psychotic disorders. Treatment: <strong>exposure and response prevention (ERP)</strong>
    — trigger the obsession, block the ritual, repeat — is among the best-supported
    psychological treatments anywhere, with response in roughly half to two-thirds of
    completers; SSRIs at higher-than-usual doses are the first-line medication. OCD
    also demonstrates the diagnosis lesson: it sits near hair-pulling and skin-picking
    disorders in DSM-5's own chapter, grouped by mechanism (ritualised escape), not by
    symptom surface.
  </p>

  <h2>Impulse control disorders: the failure to brake</h2>
  <p>
    The third corner of the clinical map inverts the anxiety story. The
    <strong>impulse control disorders</strong> (kleptomania — recurrent theft of unneeded
    items; pyromania — deliberate fire-setting for tension release; intermittent
    explosive disorder — disproportionate aggressive outbursts) share a signature:
    mounting tension before the act, pleasure or relief during, and often guilt after —
    a failure of inhibition rather than an excess of fear. <strong>Gambling
    disorder</strong> was moved into the addictions chapter of DSM-5 on the strength of
    shared mechanism: reward anticipation, chase-losses escalation, tolerance and
    withdrawal-like restlessness, and near-identical neural responses to monetary and
    drug rewards. Treatments are CBT targeting the trigger-tension-act chain and
    cognitive distortions (the gambler's fallacy, illusion of control), with opioid
    antagonists (naltrexone) showing trial evidence for kleptomania and gambling —
    parallel to their use in addictions, another hint of shared machinery.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> OCD means being tidy or organised. Clinical OCD is
  not a preference for order; it is hours of intrusive fear and ritual that the
  sufferer often hates, and colloquial "so OCD" usage hides a disabling condition —
  one reason clinicians care about precise language.</p>

  <h2>What works: the honest meta-analysis</h2>
  <p>
    Psychotherapy works: across hundreds of controlled trials the average treated
    patient ends up better off than about 75-80% of untreated controls (effect size
    around 0.8 — Smith & Glass's classic meta-analysis, repeatedly updated). The
    <strong>dodo bird verdict</strong> (Wampold) adds the uncomfortable finding: bona
    fide therapies show broadly similar <em>average</em> effects for many conditions,
    implicating common factors — the therapeutic <strong>alliance</strong> is the best
    single outcome predictor (Flückiger's meta-analysis of hundreds of studies), with
    expectancy, structure and empathy close behind. But the verdict is not a licence
    for anything-goes: for specific disorders, <em>specific</em> techniques win — ERP
    for OCD, exposure for phobias and PTSD, behavioural activation for depression,
    antipsychotics for schizophrenia's positive symptoms, lithium for bipolar
    maintenance, ECT for severe or treatment-resistant depression (effective and
    misunderstood; modern practice is anaesthetised and unilateral). Best practice is
    therefore a two-step: common factors make therapy work; specific factors make it
    work <em>for that disorder</em>. Medication and psychotherapy are complements, not
    rivals — combination beats either alone where trials have run the race.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The antidepressant placebo debate.</strong> Kirsch's reanalyses argued
    published drug-placebo differences for mild-moderate depression are small, growing
    with severity; defenders counter that averaging hides patient heterogeneity and
    unpublished trials. The live synthesis: for severe depression, antidepressants
    show clear advantage; for mild cases, watchful waiting, activation and therapy are
    defensible first steps — and the placebo component of every treatment (expectancy,
    care, ritual) is itself a mechanism psychology should own rather than apologise
    for.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The treatment gap.</strong> The WHO estimates that between 76% and 85% of
    people with mental disorders in low- and middle-income countries receive no
    treatment — the field's greatest challenge is delivery, not discovery. Task-shifting
    (training lay health workers to deliver manualised CBT) has strong trial results
    in Zimbabwe, Pakistan and India; brief digital interventions are being tested at
    scale. The next decade of clinical psychology may be decided by implementation
    science.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Will dimensional systems (HiTOP, RDoC) replace
    diagnostic categories in the clinic, or only in research? Can treatment selection
    become personalised — biomarker-guided rather than trial-and-error? Do
    hallucinogen-assisted therapies (psilocybin for depression, MDMA-assisted therapy
    for PTSD) survive their current wave of trials with effects intact — early results
    are promising and regulatory decisions have been contested? And can the
    neurodiversity framework and clinical need find a stable settlement, as the DSM-5
    attempt for autism suggests? The field's own history — homosexuality's removal,
    Rosenhan's lesson, the dimensional turn — says the categories will keep moving;
    the treatments will keep being tested.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> DSM-5-TR and ICD-11; Rosenhan (1973); Tienari et al. Finnish adoption studies; Beck (1976); Seligman (1971, 1975); Wolpe (1958); Craske et al. on inhibitory learning; Smith & Glass (1977); Wampold & Imel, The Great Psychotherapy Debate; Flückiger et al. (2018) alliance meta-analysis; Kirsch et al. (2008); Cuijpers meta-analyses; WHO World Mental Health reports.</p>

